  "description": "Range queries for coding interviews: when prefix sums stop working, the Fenwick tree (lowbit, point update, counting with compressed values), the iterative segment tree, lazy propagation in brief, and ordered-map floor and ceiling for My Calendar, with templates in Go, C++, Java and Python.",
  "hero": {
    "tag": "header",
    "attrs": {
      "class": "page-hero"
    },
    "html": "\r\n  <div class=\"hero-meta\"><span class=\"level-badge\" data-level=\"4\">Level 4 - Advanced</span><span class=\"time\">60 min</span><span class=\"chip\">Pattern primer</span><span class=\"chip\">Fenwick tree</span><span class=\"chip\">Segment tree</span><span class=\"chip\">Ordered map</span><span class=\"chip\">O(log n)</span></div>\r\n  <h1>Range queries: Fenwick tree, segment tree, ordered map</h1>\r\n  <p class=\"lead\">Prefix sums answer \"sum of a range\" in O(1), until the array changes. This page is the next step: a Fenwick tree (ten lines, point update and prefix sum in O(log n)), an iterative segment tree (any associative operation: sum, min, max, gcd), a short look at lazy propagation, and the ordered map with floor and ceiling that solves My Calendar and its cousins. It is SDE-3 depth on purpose: enough to recognise the problem, pick the structure, and write it from memory, without competitive-programming tricks. Templates in Go, C++, Java and Python.</p>\r\n"
  },
